<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2600–2000 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/27th-century-bce">27th century BCE</a> · Also known as Sacred City of Caral-Supe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caral is an archaeological site in the Supe valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, about 23 km from the coast, and the largest centre of the Norte Chico or Caral-Supe civilisation. Its platform mounds, sunken circular plazas and houses date from about 2600 BCE, making it one of the oldest cities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Excavated from 1994 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruth-shady">Ruth Shady</a>, it is a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caral.jpg" alt="Caral" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jon Gudorf Photography · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mayor_Pyramid_and_circular_enclosure._Caral,_Peru.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
